    "problem_statement": "# Variable equality comparison broken in dotenv\n\n## Description\n\nThe equality comparison (`__eq__`) method in the `Variable` class is not working correctly. This breaks variable interpolation functionality when using variables in environment files.\n\n## Steps to reproduce\n\nCreate a `.env` file with variable interpolation:\n\n```\nb=value\na=${b}\n```\n\nThen try to load it:\n\n```python\nimport dotenv\n\n# Load the .env file\ndotenv.load_dotenv()\n\n# Or try to get values directly\nvalues = dotenv.dotenv_values()\nprint(values)  # Should have 'a' with the value of 'b'\n```\n\nThe variable interpolation doesn't work correctly. The value of `a` is not being set to the value of `b` as expected.\n\n## Expected behavior\n\nWhen using variable interpolation like `${variable}` or `${variable:-default}` in a `.env` file, the variables should be correctly resolved and interpolated.\n\nFor example, with a file containing:\n```\nb=value\na=${b}\n```\n\nThe result should be:\n```\n{'b': 'value', 'a': 'value'}\n```\n\n## Actual behavior\n\nVariable interpolation is not working correctly. The variables are not being properly compared during the interpolation process, which prevents the correct substitution of values.\n\nThis affects all forms of variable interpolation including:\n- `${a}` (simple variable reference)\n- `${a:-b}` (variable with default value)\n- Multiple variables in a single value like `${a}${b}` or `prefix${a}suffix`"
  }
